WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
Why does a bacterium offered glucose and lactose postpone the lactose genes?

2
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.780
Follow one promoter inside a single cell.

3
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.200
Two signals must line up before its output becomes high.

4
00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:21.703
The rail carries the lacZ, lacY, and lacA genes after one promoter.

5
00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:26.103
Polymerase can copy them into one messenger RNA.

6
00:00:27.583 --> 00:00:32.503
Together, these genes form one operon and produce one messenger with three coding

7
00:00:32.503 --> 00:00:38.083
regions. But a red repressor clamp grips the operator beside that promoter.

8
00:00:39.263 --> 00:00:40.783
The clamp blocks transcription.

9
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:45.307
Yellow lactose crosses the membrane through permease.

10
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:51.787
β-Galactosidase turns a little lactose into allolactose, the natural inducer.

11
00:00:53.247 --> 00:00:56.547
Lactose itself is not the inducer; conversion matters.

12
00:00:58.087 --> 00:01:02.087
Allolactose reshapes the clamp and lowers its affinity for the operator.

13
00:01:03.287 --> 00:01:05.947
The clamp loosens, so polymerase can move.

14
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:08.300
This creates a loop.

15
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.680
Permease and β-galactosidase help produce the signal that increases their own

16
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:20.800
production. The loop can amplify a small beginning into stronger production when

17
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.680
conditions allow. Basal leak supplies a few starting proteins.

18
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.900
So the switch is not perfectly binary.

19
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.410
Removing the brake is only one input.

20
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:35.310
The promoter is weak by itself.

21
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:40.370
Teal CAP, carrying cyclic AMP, docks upstream.

22
00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.710
It contacts polymerase and helps transcription start.

23
00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.290
The activator does not remove repressor.

24
00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.570
Each regulator has a different job.

25
00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.210
This is positive control.

26
00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:57.307
Glucose changes both inputs.

27
00:01:58.567 --> 00:02:02.867
In the classic account, cyclic AMP is lower when glucose is plentiful.

28
00:02:04.347 --> 00:02:07.027
How much this matters in living cells is debated.

29
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:12.307
Glucose uptake also makes a transport protein block permease.

30
00:02:13.627 --> 00:02:17.507
Less lactose enters, so less allolactose releases the repressor.

31
00:02:18.617 --> 00:02:20.177
Now predict one case.

32
00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:24.417
Lactose is present, but glucose is also plentiful.

33
00:02:25.917 --> 00:02:28.917
Will the expression meter reach high, or stop below it?

34
00:02:29.997 --> 00:02:31.397
Hold both signals in view.

35
00:02:37.383 --> 00:02:41.423
The output is reduced, not high and not necessarily absent.

36
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:48.183
Some inducer may release the brake, but activation is weaker and lactose entry is

37
00:02:48.183 --> 00:02:53.143
inhibited. The two controls combine without becoming a perfect switch.

38
00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:59.427
Without lactose, the repressor keeps expression very low, even when glucose is

39
00:02:59.427 --> 00:03:03.527
scarce. With both sugars, expression is reduced.

40
00:03:04.907 --> 00:03:10.307
Only lactose with scarce glucose releases the brake and supplies strong activation

41
00:03:10.307 --> 00:03:16.167
together. This table predicts expression levels, not whether every cell behaves

42
00:03:16.167 --> 00:03:17.167
identically.

43
00:03:17.967 --> 00:03:22.607
The promoter is reading two conditions, not naming a preferred food by itself.

44
00:03:24.027 --> 00:03:29.947
Keep the scope clear: this rule describes high expression in wild-type E. coli under

45
00:03:29.947 --> 00:03:31.287
the lesson's standard model.

46
00:03:32.587 --> 00:03:36.027
In the lab, a synthetic inducer can stand in for lactose.
